course: dictionary
lesson: 12013
title: The chequebook and the cheque
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A chequebook is a small book of printed forms called cheques. A bank gives one to each customer. Context helps you see this: «Mum took the chequebook out of her bag and found a pen.» The pen and the paying show that a chequebook is something you use to pay for things.
explain: A cheque is a paper form you fill in to pay someone from your bank account. You write the amount in numbers and in words, then sign your name. «She wrote the amount on the cheque, signed it, and gave it to the builder.» The clues are amount, signed and gave, so a cheque is a way of paying.
explain: Fewer people use chequebooks now, because cards and phones do most of the paying. Some people still use them for bills or gifts. «Grandma sent a cheque for my birthday, tucked inside the card.» Word clues help, but a dictionary tells you the exact meaning when you are not sure.
fact: A chequebook is a small book of printed cheques that a bank gives to a customer.
fact: A cheque is used to pay someone from your bank account.
fact: On a cheque you write the amount in numbers and words, then sign it.
fact: «Chequebook» is spelled «checkbook» in American English.
fact: In an informal sense, «chequebook» means paying money to solve a problem, as in «chequebook journalism».
exercise: «She took a chequebook from her bag to pay the plumber.» What is a chequebook? | A card you tap in a shop | A book of stories about money | *A small book of cheques that a bank gives you | A bag for keeping coins
exercise: «He wrote the amount on the cheque.» On a cheque, what do you write the amount in? | Pictures only | Colours | Emojis | *Numbers and words
exercise: In American English, chequebook is spelled differently. Which spelling is used? | chequebok | checquebook | chequebooks-two | *checkbook
test: «Dad wrote a cheque from his chequebook to pay for the new fence.» Where did the cheque come from? | His phone | *His chequebook | The shop's till | The garden
test: Which phrase from the lessons means writing a payment form? | swipe a card | *write a cheque | tap a watch | scan a code
exam: Why are chequebooks used less now? | Nobody has ever wanted to pay by cheque | Banks have banned them | *Cards and phones do most of the paying | They are too heavy to carry
